Add a Split-View mode to the editor, alongside the existing source code, typewriter, and focus modes. In Split-View, the document is shown as two panes side by side: one pane where the user edits the document as plain Markdown text, and a second pane that continuously displays the rendered, formatted result of that text. The rendered pane is not directly editable — all editing happens in the text pane, and the rendered pane reflects it automatically as the user types, without requiring a manual refresh or a mode switch to see the update.

Split-View is selectable from the View menu the same way the other view modes are, and behaves consistently with them: selecting it doesn't alter the document's content, and switching to any other view mode and back to Split-View preserves whatever was written, exactly as switching between the existing modes already does today. Only one view mode is active at a time, consistent with how the current modes work.

The rendered pane must accurately reflect standard Markdown formatting — headings, emphasis, lists, links, code blocks, and the other constructs the editor already supports elsewhere — so that what appears there matches what the user would see in the app's normal rendered view of the same content. A brief, reasonable delay between typing and the rendered pane updating is acceptable; the update does not need to be character-by-character instantaneous.
